<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<cfsSimulation xmlns="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation"
xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation
https://opencfs.gitlab.io/cfs/xml/CFS-Simulation/CFS.xsd">

  <documentation>
    <title>Analytic python volume constraint with exact Hessian via the 'hessian' attribute</title>
    <authors>
      <author>Fabian Wein</author>
    </authors>
    <date>2026-07-31</date>
    <references>no</references>
    <isVerified>no</isVerified>
    <description>
      We have here a test for an analytic function, nonlinearly based on shape variables, not via density.

      We also test to have multiple python functions in the same kernel (one master, the other imported)

      We also test compliance_py with a Python state dependent function and exact Hessian.

      The test content is the 'hessian' attribute of the python function element: the analytic
      capsule volume from share/python/pill_volume.py is defined directly on the feature variables
      (no density representation), so the density based 'curvature' interface cannot express its
      Hessian. vol_hessian returns the exact Hessian in the full feature variable space and
      Function::CalcShapeHessianPython / FeatureMappingDesign::CalcShapeHessian reduce it to the
      optimization variables - the same convention as the feature space gradient. With
      fd_check="true" the driver asserts the full objective Hessian AND the volume constraint
      Hessian row against central differences of the Jacobian.

      The global python element merges observed_objective.py into the pill_volume.py kernel via
      the import mechanism (from module import *).
    </description>
  </documentation>

  <!-- kernel: analytic volume + the wrapped observation objective merged via import -->
  <python file="pill_volume.py" path="cfs:share:python">
    <import file="observed_objective.py" path="cfs:share:python"/>
  </python>

  <fileFormats>
    <input>
      <regular geometryType="plane" region="mech">
        <box xMax="1.0" yMax="1.0"/>
        <elements nx="16"/>
      </regular>
    </input>
    <output>
      <hdf5/>
      <info/>
    </output>
    <materialData file="mat.xml" format="xml"/>
  </fileFormats>

  <domain geometryType="plane">
    <regionList>
      <region material="99lines" name="mech"/>
    </regionList>
  </domain>

  <sequenceStep index="1">
    <analysis>
      <static/>
    </analysis>

    <pdeList>
      <mechanic subType="planeStress">
        <regionList>
          <region name="mech"/>
        </regionList>

        <!-- cantilever: clamped left edge, tip load at the lower right corner -->
        <bcsAndLoads>
           <fix name="left">
              <comp dof="x"/>
              <comp dof="y"/>
           </fix>
           <force name="bottom_right">
             <comp dof="y" value="-1"/>
           </force>
        </bcsAndLoads>
        <storeResults>
          <elemResult type="mechPseudoDensity">
            <allRegions/>
          </elemResult>
        </storeResults>
      </mechanic>
    </pdeList>
  </sequenceStep>

  <optimization>
    <costFunction type="python" task="minimize">
      <python name="compliance_py" init="obj_init" eval="obj_eval" grad="obj_grad" curvature="obj_curvature" script="kernel">
        <option key="observation" value="compliance"/>
      </python>
    </costFunction>

    <!-- the wrapped C++ compliance: evaluated and differentiated on demand from the python objective -->
    <constraint type="compliance" mode="observation" observeGradient="true"/>

    <!-- the analytic capsule volume with exact Hessian, both directly on the feature variables -->
    <constraint type="python" value="0.35" bound="upperBound">
      <python name="volume" init="vol_init" eval="vol_eval" grad="vol_grad" hessian="vol_hessian" script="kernel"/>
    </constraint>

    <!-- remember that scipy's trust-region method is not meaningful in reality - but we have its FD test.
         For real use take the native Hessian IPOPT (see hessian_compliance) or hessian_ipopt.py -->
    <optimizer type="python" maxIterations="50">
      <python file="hessian_scipy.py" path="cfs:share:python">
        <option key="observation" value="compliance"/>
        <option key="fd_check" value="true"/>
        <option key="gtol" value="1e-4"/>
        <option key="xtol" value="1e-9"/>
        <option key="initial_tr_radius" value="0.1"/>
      </python>
    </optimizer>

    <!-- boundary is the C^2 quintic; the exact geometric Hessian needs H'' -->
    <ersatzMaterial region="mech" material="mechanic" method="featureMapping">
      <featureMapping transition="0.1" boundary="quintic" order="3" combine="p-norm" p="3" hessexport="true">
        <pill>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.05" upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.1"  upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.95" upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.1"  upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <profile initial="0.08" lower="0.05" upper="0.25"/>
        </pill>
        <pill>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.05" upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.9"  upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.95" upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.15" upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <profile initial="0.08" lower="0.05" upper="0.25"/>
        </pill>
      </featureMapping>
      <design name="density" initial=".5" physical_lower="1e-3" upper="1.0"/>

      <transferFunction type="identity" application="mech"/>
      <export save="last" write="iteration"/>
    </ersatzMaterial>
    <commit mode="forward" stride="99"/>
  </optimization>
</cfsSimulation>
